==Old English==

===Etymology===
{{etyl|gem-pro|ang}} {{recons|newun|lang=gem-pro}}, from {{proto|ine-pro|h₁néwn̥|nine|lang=ang}}.  Cognate with Old Frisian [[nigun]] (West Frisian [[njoggen]]), Old Saxon [[nigun]] (Dutch [[negen]]), together representing a variant of the original Germanic (with /g/ for /w/), seen in Old High German [[niun]] (German [[neun]]), Old Norse ''níu'' (Swedish [[nio]]), Gothic [[𐌽𐌹𐌿𐌽]].  The IE source also led to Latin [[novem]], Old Irish ''nōi'' (Irish [[naoi]]), Old Slavic ''devęt’'' (Russian [[девять]]).

